From 0000000000000000000000000000000000000000 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Christophe Pettus <xof@thebuild.com>
Date: Thu, 1 Oct 2026 23:28:40 -0400
Subject: [PATCH 16/22] H10e: count fonts that are replaced by a substitute

When a font cannot be loaded, hayro keeps its text and draws it with a
standard font: a Type1, TrueType or CID font whose embedded program
cannot be used falls back to a standard or fallback font inside its
constructor, and a font resource that does not describe a font at all
(a reference to nothing, a non-dictionary, an unknown or missing
`/Subtype`, a Type0 font without `/DescendantFonts`) is replaced by
Helvetica. Nothing told the caller that this happened, although the
glyph shapes and metrics are then not the document's.

Each such substitution now bumps a thread-local counter, read with
`font_substitutions()`:

- in `Type1Font::new`, `TrueTypeFont::new` and `Type0Font::new` when the
  font descriptor has a `/FontFile`, `/FontFile2` or `/FontFile3` that
  cannot be used and a substitute is built in its place;
- in `Context::resolve_font` when `Font::new` returns `None` on loading
  (fonts are cached per `InterpreterCache`, so a broken font is counted
  once, when it is loaded);
- in the `Tf` handler when the font name is present in the resources
  but does not resolve to a dictionary (nothing can be cached, so this
  counts each selection).

A font that is simply not embedded is not counted, and neither is a
font name missing from the resources, for which Helvetica is the
intended behaviour. Drawing is unchanged.
